  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nUpper trough is expected to dig over the Pacific NW Friday that is\nexpected to close off over Oregon Saturday then drift into \nwestern Montana Sunday.\n\nDiurnal waves of showers and thunderstorms will move in from the\nSW, the first one today will move into the SW zones late this\nafternoon per HRRR. Some strong winds are possible. They diminish\nas they move into the eastern zones later tonight.\n\nOn Friday, the next wave of thunderstorms will move in during the\nevening from the SW. The best severe potential looks to stay just\nsouth of the CWA with a Marginal Risk outlined by SPC. With higher\nhumidity/Precip Water, hail and wind appears to be equal threats.\n\nOn Saturday, models remain uncertain with severe potential. Higher\npotential could be south of the CWA where jet energy and\ninstability look to be more ideal. SPC has a Slight Risk over all\nbut the western portion of the CWA.\n\nPrecipitable waters and surface dewpoints will be on the rise\nespecially over the eastern zones into Saturday but there could be\nenough capping that heavy rain may not be too much of a concern.\nTotal storm amounts were lowered in the eastern zones with this\nforecast package.\n\nTo start out July, the latest GFS and ECMWF ensemble have \ntemperatures climbing warmer than normal and precipitation coming \ndown, so the sprinklers may start coming in handy again. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nIncreased winds today and early this evening, following the NBM90.\nAlso blended in some near-term/CAMS for pops through this evening.\nTFJ",
